  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 258 AM CDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nBy Tuesday and Wednesday, the upper-level ridge that had been in \nplace across the region last week, shifts back eastward, quickly \nretreating the surface high pressure and enabling low-level \nsouthwesterly WAA that warms 850-hPa temperatures back to 21 to 23 C \n(95-99th climatological percentile). As a result, high temperatures \nwill once again warm into the mid-90s to around 100 F, nearing daily \nrecords. Fortunately, humidity/dewpoints will be slow to increase \nthrough Tuesday. On Wednesday, a cold front is progged to advance \nsouthward into the CWA, favoring a more substantial increase in \ndewpoints with more time for advection and moisture pooling near the \nfront. The trade off is that this front makes temperatures less \nclear, especially north of I-70. To the south ahead of the front, \nfavorable placement of the low-level thermal ridge and west-\nsouthwesterly flow may actually support Wednesday being the hottest \nday of this shorter heat period in that area. There, it will not be \ndifficult for heat index values to at least flirt with 100 F, but \nprobabilities of 105+ F heat index values are 5 percent or less in \nglobal ensemble model guidance. Although these probabilities are \nlikely underdone, it is not yet clear if heat index values will \nexceed 105 F outside of a sporadic/localized basis (single day Heat \nAdvisory Criteria).\n\nEnsemble model 24-hour probabilities of measurable rainfall with \nshowers and thunderstorms increase to 60 to 90 percent Wednesday \nevening with the cold front, persisting through late Thursday as \nfrontogenetical forcing lingers behind the front that eventually \nadvances south of the CWA in a quasi-zonal upper-level flow regime. \nThe post-frontal airmass, cloud cover, and precipitation support \nThursday being the coolest day of the week with high temperatures in \nthe 80s F, but it is conceivable that temperatures even remain in \nthe 70s F if/where clouds and precipitation are persistent.\n\nFriday through next weekend, global model guidance is in agreement \nthat the upper-level wave pattern will amplify across the CONUS but \nensemble model 500-hPa height cluster analysis reveals that there is \nconsiderable disagreement on the timing of an eastward propagating \nupper-level trough or if it becomes cutoff near the Pacific \nNorthwest and a downstream ridge dominates much of the CONUS. This \nrange of scenarios leads to agreement in at least some warming of \ntemperatures further above average into the weekend, but the NBM \ninterquartile range increases to 15 F by Sunday with uncertainty if \nor when there is a cold frontal passage. Similarly, ensemble model \nmembership shows very little cohesion with regard to rainfall, \nleading to broad-brushed (in time and space) lower probabilities of \nmeasurable rainfall that continue through the weekend.",
